{
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "score": 3,
  "estimated_minutes": 25,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The reviewer needs only the basic distributor relationship and Premier’s request for a preliminary injunction against Kellogg’s 2014 termination on 90 days’ notice."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The scope of “Subject to Section 12” and the reconciliation of termination rights require familiar contract principles, not snack-distribution expertise."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Understanding the construction requires reading Section 2 alongside Section 12(i) and the five-business-day cure provisions in Section 12(iii), with the 2008 language providing supplementary context."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The court expressly identifies both readings and adopts Kellogg’s distinction between termination before and after December 31, 2010, while clearly labeling its extrinsic-evidence discussion as alternative reasoning."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The defects share the same construction dispute, so the reviewer must distinguish modifier scope in d01 from the reciprocal claims that either Section 2 or Section 12 becomes superfluous in d02 and d03."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d03 — The reviewer must look beyond Section 12(i), which itself specifies 90 days’ notice, to Section 12(iii)’s shorter cure-based termination mechanism to understand the court’s harmonization example. This also requires distinguishing Premier’s argument that Section 12 becomes superfluous from the reverse superfluity argument in d02.",
  "reason": "This is suitable for a general contract reviewer without industry-specific preparation. The central question is straightforward: whether “Subject to Section 12” restricts termination throughout the relationship or only through December 31, 2010. A targeted reading of Sections 2 and 12 and opinion lines 75–110 should establish both the construction and its supporting reasoning. The main care needed is to keep the reciprocal superfluity arguments separate and recognize that the court’s five-day example comes from Section 12(iii), rather than Section 12(i)."
}
